From 9eeb23d75be9ac64fd8bda834cdf8ac7b84ced39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joshua Bryan <45343084+rafe-walker@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:46:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] feat(plugin-mcp): batch-friendly preconditions:
 per-document checks in bulk update and delete (research v2)

The research build's instructions told agents to pass ifUpdatedAt on every update and delete. A where-based bulk
call cannot carry one version per document, so agents fell back to one call per document (U11 batching 8/10 on the
vendor build, 0/10 on research v1). The read-version precondition already covers documents the agent has read, so
the explicit rule was redundant.

- Instructions: several documents getting the same change or deletion go in ONE call with where; every update and
  delete, bulk included, is checked per document against the version the user last read; ifUpdatedAt only pins a
  specific version (a string with id, a map with where).
- ifUpdatedAt accepts a per-document version map {"<id>": "<updatedAt>"}; a map alone selects the documents it names.
  A single string with a where call is refused (nothing written).
- Bulk update and delete check every matching document first (pinned version, else the last-read version, scoped by
  createdAt as for by-id writes): stale documents are refused with their current state, the rest are written.
  The answer is best-effort and says so (mode best_effort, applied and not_changed counts, per-document results);
  headline plain, PARTIAL: (some applied) or Conflict: (none applied); isError when any document was not changed.

Best-effort like the by-id check (read, then write); a true compare-and-set needs core support.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
